WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.832
Secrets are values that grant access, prove identity, decrypt data, sign operations, or connect one system to another.

2
00:00:06.832 --> 00:00:14.424
In C++ applications they can appear in backend services, desktop software, command-line tools, embedded devices, agents, plugins, and shared

3
00:00:14.424 --> 00:00:14.803
libraries.

4
00:00:14.803 --> 00:00:25.431
A program can be careful about memory management and still create serious risk if it logs an API key, embeds a credential in a binary, or stores a

5
00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:28.848
private key in a file with weak access controls.

6
00:00:28.848 --> 00:00:32.643
Secure secret storage is not only about encrypting a string.

7
00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:41.374
It is the full lifecycle of the secret: how the value is created, delivered, accessed, used, monitored, rotated, revoked, and eventually removed from

8
00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.133
operational systems.

9
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:44.031
Each stage can introduce exposure.

10
00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:53.900
A secret may be safe in a managed store but unsafe when copied into a command line, crash dump, support bundle, test fixture, or build artifact.

11
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.695
Defensive engineering looks at the path, not just the container.

12
00:00:57.695 --> 00:01:01.491
The goal is to make safe handling the normal path.

13
00:01:01.491 --> 00:01:09.083
Teams should identify which values are secrets, classify their scope, choose approved stores, control delivery, keep runtime exposure short, redact

14
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:12.878
logs, and plan rotation before an incident forces the issue.

15
00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:20.849
The decision should be visible in design notes, deployment configuration, and code review so the next maintainer understands the intended path.

16
00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:28.061
This course focuses on practical C++ engineering habits: clear ownership, platform-aware storage, careful runtime use, least privilege, and

17
00:01:28.061 --> 00:01:29.200
reviewable lifecycle rules.
